window.AFC_TOPICS.push(
  {
    id: "dcf",
    title: "DCF Valuation",
    description: "From cash flows to enterprise value and equity value.",
    source: "Lectures 12 and 14 · Value measurement: DCF methods. Lecture 17 · Exercises on FCFF, FCFE, cost of capital and terminal value.",
    notes: [
      {title: "Choose who receives the flow", body: "FCFF rewards all capital providers. Discount it at the WACC to estimate enterprise value. FCFE belongs to shareholders and is discounted at the cost of equity. In the course’s simplified bridge, subtract net financial debt from enterprise value to obtain equity value."},
      {title: "From EBIT to cash", body: "Start from EBIT after notional operating taxes. Add back depreciation and amortisation, because they reduced profit without a payment in the period. Subtract the increase in operating working capital and net capital expenditure. A fall in inventories, other items being equal, releases cash."},
      {title: "The FCFE bridge in the AFC exercises", body: "To FCFF, add financial income after tax and subtract financial expenses net of the tax shield. Add new debt net of repayments. The lecture schema also adds paid-in capital increases and subtracts capital repayments and dividends. Distinguish these cash transactions from a mere change in book equity."},
      {title: "Estimate the target’s risk", body: "With the CAPM, the market premium is the difference between the expected market return and the risk-free rate. For an unlisted target, remove the effect of leverage from the beta of each comparable company, average the unlevered betas and apply the target’s planned leverage. Use the currency of the cash flows to choose the risk-free rate."},
      {title: "Place the terminal value on the timeline", body: "The terminal value at the end of year T includes the flows from year T+1 onwards. A growing perpetuity requires sustainable growth and a discount rate above g. For an asset with a defined end date, discount only the remaining years and consider any disposal value at the expected date."}
    ],
    formulas: [
      {name: "Free cash flow to the firm", expression: "FCFF = EBIT × (1 − t) + D&A − ΔNOWC − net capex", detail: "NOWC = trade receivables + inventories − trade payables in the simplified schema. Δ means closing minus opening balance. Net capex equals payments for fixed assets minus proceeds from disposals."},
      {name: "Flow to shareholders under the AFC schema", expression: "FCFE = FCFF + (financial income − financial expenses) × (1 − t) + Δdebt + Δpaid-in capital − dividends", detail: "Debt and capital movements include proceeds net of repayments. The bridge assumes the same tax rate on financial income and expenses."},
      {name: "Equity beta", expression: "βL = βU × [1 + (1 − t) × D/E]", detail: "To remove leverage, divide the levered beta by the factor in brackets. Use the leverage and tax rate of the company each beta belongs to."},
      {name: "Cost of equity", expression: "ke = rf + βL × (rm − rf)", detail: "rm is the expected market return. If the figure given is already a market risk premium, multiply it directly by βL."},
      {name: "Weighted average cost of capital", expression: "WACC = ke × E/(D + E) + kd × (1 − t) × D/(D + E)", detail: "For a market-value valuation, E and D are the market values of equity and financial debt. In exercises, follow the basis given: Lecture 17, exercise 1, uses book-value weights. If kd is already after tax, the tax shield is already included."},
      {name: "Growing perpetuity", expression: "TV(T) = F(T+1)/(k − g)", detail: "The first flow after the plan is F(T+1) = F(T) × (1 + g). It requires k > g and an infinite horizon."},
      {name: "Level finite annuity", expression: "TV(T) = F × [1 − (1 + k)⁻ⁿ]/k", detail: "The first of the n payments falls one year after T. If the business ends, consider only the remaining expected payments."},
      {name: "Present value of the plan", expression: "V(0) = Σ F(t)/(1 + k)ᵗ + TV(T)/(1 + k)ᵀ", detail: "Add up the future flows from year 1 to year T. Any flow at time zero enters directly if it is within the scope being valued."}
    ],
    questions: [
      {id:"dcf-01",type:"choice",level:1,prompt:"The plan forecasts flows available to both creditors and shareholders. Which pair gives the operating value of the whole firm?",options:["FCFE and WACC","FCFF and WACC","FCFF and cost of equity","Dividends and cost of debt"],answer:1,explanation:"FCFF is the flow available before debt service. The WACC combines the returns required by the two classes of capital providers and keeps flows and rate consistent."},
      {id:"dcf-02",type:"number",level:2,context:"A manufacturer forecasts EBIT of 120 million, a 25% tax rate, depreciation of 18 million, an increase in operating working capital of 11 million, payments for new plant of 37 million and disposal proceeds of 5 million, with no gains or losses on disposal.",prompt:"What is the year’s FCFF?",unit:"million €",answer:65,tolerance:0.01,explanation:"EBIT after tax is 120 × 0.75 = 90. Net capex is 37 − 5 = 32. The flow is 90 + 18 − 11 − 32 = 65 million. The increase in working capital absorbs cash."},
      {id:"dcf-03",type:"multi",level:1,prompt:"Other conditions being equal, which changes increase the year’s FCFF?",options:["A fall in trade receivables","An increase in inventories","An increase in trade payables","Paying a larger dividend"],answer:[0,2],explanation:"Collecting receivables and deferring payments to suppliers reduce operating working capital. Growing inventories absorb resources. The dividend is a distribution decision that comes after FCFF."},
      {id:"dcf-04",type:"number",level:2,context:"In the course’s simplified schema, net fixed assets go from 360 to 395 million. Depreciation is 27 million. The only other movement is the purchase of plant.",prompt:"What capex should be subtracted when calculating FCFF?",unit:"million €",answer:62,tolerance:0.01,explanation:"The closing balance equals the opening balance plus purchases minus depreciation. Purchases are therefore 395 − 360 + 27 = 62 million."},
      {id:"dcf-05",type:"number",level:3,context:"FCFF of 64 million. Financial income 4, interest expense 12, tax rate 25%, new loans 20, loan repayments 9, paid-in capital increase 6, dividends paid 8. Amounts in € million.",prompt:"Apply the FCFF–FCFE bridge from the AFC lectures. What is FCFE?",unit:"million €",answer:67,tolerance:0.01,explanation:"Net financial items after tax are (4 − 12) × 0.75 = −6. Debt contributes 20 − 9 = 11. FCFE under the AFC schema is 64 − 6 + 11 + 6 − 8 = 67."},
      {id:"dcf-06",type:"number",level:1,context:"The risk-free rate is 2.5%, the expected market return 7.5% and the levered beta 1.30.",prompt:"Estimate the cost of equity with the CAPM.",unit:"%",answer:9,tolerance:0.01,explanation:"The market premium is 7.5% − 2.5% = 5%. The required return is therefore 2.5% + 1.30 × 5% = 9%."},
      {id:"dcf-07",type:"order",level:2,prompt:"An unlisted company needs to estimate its equity beta. Order the steps from selecting peers to the final estimate.",options:["Calculate the average unlevered beta","Apply the target’s leverage and tax rate to the average beta","Select listed companies with similar operating risk","Strip each peer’s beta of its financial leverage"],answer:[2,3,0,1],explanation:"You start from companies with comparable activities. Removing each one’s leverage makes the betas comparable for averaging. The target’s factor [1 + (1 − t) × D/E] is then applied to the average."},
      {id:"dcf-08",type:"number",level:2,context:"A peer has a levered beta of 1.45, debt/equity of 0.60 and a 25% tax rate. The target runs the same business, with debt/equity of 0.40 and a 20% tax rate.",prompt:"What is the target’s levered beta under the comparables method?",unit:"β",answer:1.32,tolerance:0.005,explanation:"The peer’s βU = 1.45/[1 + 0.75 × 0.60] = 1. For the target, βL = 1 × [1 + 0.80 × 0.40] = 1.32."},
      {id:"dcf-09",type:"number",level:2,context:"Invested capital is financed 60% by equity and 40% by debt. The cost of equity is 10%, the pre-tax cost of debt is 5% and the tax rate is 25%.",prompt:"Calculate the WACC.",unit:"%",answer:7.5,tolerance:0.01,explanation:"Debt costs 5% × 0.75 = 3.75% after tax. The two weighted contributions are 0.60 × 10% = 6% and 0.40 × 3.75% = 1.5%. The WACC is 7.5%."},
      {id:"dcf-10",type:"choice",level:1,prompt:"What does an estimated equity beta of 1.4 mean in the CAPM?",options:["Sales grow by 1.4% every year","The cost of debt is 40% above the average","The stock’s return is more than proportionally sensitive to the market","The stock’s total volatility is always exactly 1.4 times the market’s"],answer:2,explanation:"Beta is the slope of the relationship between the stock’s return and the market return. It measures exposure to systematic risk. Total volatility also includes the company-specific component."},
      {id:"dcf-11",type:"number",level:2,context:"FCFF at the end of the last explicit year is 40 million. From the following period it will grow by 2% a year in perpetuity. The WACC is 8%.",prompt:"Calculate the terminal value at the end of the last explicit year.",unit:"million €",answer:680,tolerance:0.01,explanation:"The first FCFF of the perpetuity is 40 × 1.02 = 40.8. At the end of the plan the value is 40.8/(0.08 − 0.02) = 680 million. Bringing it back to today requires further discounting."},
      {id:"dcf-12",type:"choice",level:2,prompt:"A concession’s plan ends in year 5. Four equal annual receipts then remain and the concession expires with no disposal value. How do you value the tail at year 5?",options:["With a zero-growth perpetuity","With a finite annuity of four payments, the first in year 6","With a finite annuity of five payments, the first in year 5","With the value of the fourth payment only"],answer:1,explanation:"At the end of year 5 the receipts of years 6, 7, 8 and 9 remain. Add up their present values at year 5. The year-5 flow already belongs to the explicit plan."},
      {id:"dcf-13",type:"number",level:3,context:"The concession generates 22 million at the end of each of the three years after the end of the plan. It then expires with no residual receipts. The rate is 10%.",prompt:"What is the annuity worth at the end of the plan? Round to two decimals.",unit:"million €",answer:54.71,tolerance:0.01,explanation:"Discount the three payments to the same date: 22/1.10 + 22/1.10² + 22/1.10³ = 54.71 million. The finite life requires only three terms."},
      {id:"dcf-14",type:"multi",level:2,prompt:"Which checks should you make before applying a growing perpetuity to FCFF?",options:["The WACC must exceed the perpetual growth rate","The flows must already be net of debt repayments","Long-term growth must be sustainable","The first flow included in the terminal value must fall after the explicit plan"],answer:[0,2,3],explanation:"The model converges with WACC > g and assumes a sustainable steady state. The numerator is FCFF(T+1). Debt repayments belong to financing flows and come in through the bridge to equity."},
      {id:"dcf-15",type:"number",level:3,context:"The target will generate FCFE of 53 million in year 1 and 58 in year 2. From year 3, FCFE will grow by 2% in perpetuity. Shareholders’ required return is 10%. All flows arrive at year-end.",prompt:"Calculate today’s equity value, to two decimals.",unit:"million €",answer:707.27,tolerance:0.02,explanation:"TV(2) = 58 × 1.02/(0.10 − 0.02) = 739.50. At time zero you get 53/1.10 + (58 + 739.50)/1.10² = 707.27 million. FCFE gives the equity value directly."},
      {id:"dcf-16",type:"choice",level:3,prompt:"Why does a DCF estimate with a WACC of 7.0% and perpetual growth of 6.8% need special care?",options:["The terminal value always turns negative","The first flow beyond the plan is worth zero","Debt has to be entered twice in the model","Small changes in the rates produce large changes in the terminal value"],answer:3,explanation:"The WACC − g spread is only 0.2 percentage points, so the flow is divided by 0.002. A slight rise in the WACC or a fall in g therefore changes the value a lot, even with the same initial FCFF."},
      {id:"dcf-17",type:"number",level:2,context:"Financial debt goes from 90 to 102 million solely through new borrowing and repayments. FCFE under the AFC schema is 42 million, interest expense 8 and the tax rate 25%. Financial income, capital transactions and dividends are zero.",prompt:"Rebuild FCFF from FCFE.",unit:"million €",answer:36,tolerance:0.01,explanation:"Interest after the tax benefit is 6. Debt increased by 12. From the bridge 42 = FCFF − 6 + 12 you get FCFF = 36 million."},
      {id:"dcf-18",type:"number",level:3,context:"The flow in the last plan year is 15 million. Only three further flows will follow, each growing by 3% on the previous year; the business will then cease with no residual value. Discount rate 8%.",prompt:"What is the tail of flows worth at the end of the plan? Round to two decimals.",unit:"million €",answer:40.96,tolerance:0.01,explanation:"The three receipts are 15 × 1.03, 15 × 1.03² and 15 × 1.03³. Discounting them for one, two and three years respectively at 8% gives a total of 40.96 million."},
      {id:"dcf-19",type:"choice",exam:true,level:3,context:"Company Alpha, 2020 (k€): EBIT 4,000; D&A 1,500; change in receivables, inventories and payables (initial − final) −500; net capex 3,000; financial expenses 100; financial income 20; new debt 400; debt repaid 200; new capital issued 0; dividends paid 300; FCFE 840.",prompt:"Using the course FCFE schema, what was the income tax rate in 2020?",options:["Around 25.00%","Around 32.65%","Around 26.50%","None of the other answers"],answer:0,explanation:"FCFE = EBIT × (1 − t) + D&A + ΔNWC (i − f) − net capex − (financial expenses − financial income) × (1 − t) + new debt − debt repaid + new capital − dividends. So 840 = (4,000 − 80) × (1 − t) + 1,500 − 500 − 3,000 + 400 − 200 − 300, i.e. 3,920 × (1 − t) = 2,940 and t = 25%. Leaving out dividends gives 32.65%; forgetting the financial items gives 26.50%."},
      {id:"dcf-20",type:"choice",exam:true,level:3,context:"2025 plan (€m): financial income 50; financial expenses 120; income taxes 90; net profit 210; new loans 60; no debt repayments; dividends paid 100; no capital increase. FCFE is not expected to grow, ke is 8% and the terminal value of equity at the end of 2025 (perpetuity with no growth) is €2,500m. No data are available on NWC, D&A and capex.",prompt:"What is the FCFF for 2025?",options:["Around €289m","Around €189m","Around €310m","None of the other answers"],answer:0,explanation:"With no growth, TVE = FCFE / ke, so FCFE = 2,500 × 8% = 200. Tax rate = 90 / (210 + 90) = 30%. Going back up the bridge: FCFF = FCFE + financial expenses × (1 − t) − financial income × (1 − t) + dividends − new loans = 200 + 84 − 35 + 100 − 60 = 289. Forgetting dividends gives 189; ignoring taxes on the financial items gives 310."},
      {id:"dcf-21",type:"choice",exam:true,level:2,context:"Company A forecasts FCFE of €50,000k in year 1, €44,000k in year 2 and €48,400k in year 3. Reliable forecasts stop at year 3; afterwards FCFE stays at the year-3 level forever (perpetuity with no growth). ke is 10% and constant. Round calculations to two decimals.",prompt:"What is the equity value of Company A today?",options:["Around €481,818k","Around €118,182k","Around €602,182k","None of the other answers"],answer:0,explanation:"TVE at year 3 = 48,400 / 10% = 484,000. Discounting at ke: 50,000 / 1.1 + 44,000 / 1.1² + 48,400 / 1.1³ + 484,000 / 1.1³ = 45,454.55 + 36,363.64 + 36,363.64 + 363,636.36 ≈ 481,818. Leaving out the terminal value gives 118,182; adding it undiscounted gives 602,182."},
      {id:"dcf-22",type:"choice",exam:true,level:2,context:"FCFF is expected to be €100m in year 1 and €120m in year 2. From year 3 it will grow by 2% a year forever. The WACC is 9% and constant, and net financial debt today is €400m. Round calculations to two decimals.",prompt:"What equity value does the asset-side DCF give?",options:["Around €1,264m","Around €1,664m","Around €1,236m","None of the other answers"],answer:0,explanation:"TV at year 2 = 120 × 1.02 / (9% − 2%) = 1,748.57. EV = 100 / 1.09 + (120 + 1,748.57) / 1.09² = 1,664.48. Equity = EV − net financial debt = 1,264.48. 1,664 stops at the EV; 1,236 comes from a TV that forgets the growth factor (120 / 7%)."},
      {id:"dcf-23",type:"choice",exam:true,level:1,prompt:"All else being equal, which statement about a company’s equity value is CORRECT?",options:["Equity value does not depend on the cost of debt, because shareholders’ flows are discounted at ke","The cost of debt affects equity value: in the asset-side approach it enters the WACC used to discount FCFF","The cost of debt affects equity value because it changes the FCFF","None of the other answers"],answer:1,explanation:"FCFF is measured before any financing flow, so kd does not change it. kd enters the WACC: a higher kd raises the WACC, lowers EV and therefore lowers equity = EV − net debt. On the equity side, kd shows up as interest paid, which reduces FCFE."},
      {id:"dcf-24",type:"choice",exam:true,level:1,prompt:"The CFO wants to use a DCF valuation every year to assess each business unit manager. What is the main limitation of this choice?",options:["A DCF looks only at past results, so it cannot capture future performance","A DCF summarises the value of the whole firm, so it hardly isolates what each unit manager is responsible for","A DCF cannot be used by companies with more than one business unit","None of the other answers"],answer:1,explanation:"A DCF is forward-looking, but its value depends on many firm-wide assumptions and cash flows. That makes it a weak tool for internal accountability: it hardly separates what each unit manager actually controls."},
      {id:"dcf-25",type:"choice",exam:true,level:3,context:"2024 plan (€m): EBIT 12; tax rate 25%; D&A 6; trade receivables rise from 20 to 22, inventories fall from 5 to 4.5 and trade payables rise from 12 to 14.4; net capex 8; financial income 1.4; financial expenses 2.4; debt repaid 3; no new capital; dividends 2; FCFE 4.5.",prompt:"How much new financial debt is raised in 2024? Round to two decimals.",options:["Around €2.35m","Around €4.15m","Around €2.60m","None of the other answers"],answer:0,explanation:"NOWC goes from 20 + 5 − 12 = 13 to 22 + 4.5 − 14.4 = 12.1, releasing 0.9. FCFF = 12 × 0.75 + 6 + 0.9 − 8 = 7.9. FCFE = 7.9 + 1.4 × 0.75 − 2.4 × 0.75 + new debt − 3 − 2 = 2.15 + new debt = 4.5, so new debt = 2.35. Treating the fall in NOWC as an absorption gives 4.15; leaving the financial items before tax gives 2.60."}
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "multiples",
    title: "Relative Valuation",
    description: "Choosing comparables, market multiples and valuing divisions.",
    source: "Lecture 15 · Relative Valuation. Lecture 16 · Exercises on Relative Valuation, peer selection and business-unit valuation.",
    notes: [
      {title: "Define the peer group", body: "Identify what drives value at the target and at potential comparables. Consider customers, revenue model, markets, growth rates, risk and capital intensity. Two companies classified in the same industry can have very different economics if one manufactures and the other licenses."},
      {title: "Choose the numerator before the denominator", body: "EV belongs to creditors and shareholders. It can be related to sales, EBIT, EBITDA or FCFF. Equity value belongs to shareholders and is compared with the net profit attributable to them, the book value of equity or FCFE. Apply the same unit, scope and period to every company."},
      {title: "See what the multiple leaves out", body: "EV/Sales can still be calculated with negative margins, but two companies with the same sales can have different profits. EV/EBITDA softens the effect of depreciation policies and calls for a check on the investment needed. P/E is sensitive to leverage, taxation and one-off results. Profit close to zero can inflate it."},
      {title: "Check periods and bases across the sample", body: "A trailing multiple uses results for the last twelve months. A forward multiple uses expected results. Remove one-off items consistently and apply to the target the metric for the same period. As in the course solutions, first calculate each peer’s ratio rounded to two decimals, then take the simple average of the ratios, also rounded to two decimals."},
      {title: "Value different businesses separately", body: "For a company with autonomous divisions, give each division a homogeneous peer group and a consistent metric. Bring the results to the same level, EV or equity, before adding them up. If you add up the divisions’ operating EVs, subtract the consolidated net financial debt only once, taking into account the scope of the case."},
      {title: "Pick the multiple from the value driver", body: "First screen the potential peers on business model and competitive advantage, risk (D/E), growth and size or cash generation, and drop the outliers. Then let the target’s value driver choose the multiple: asset-heavy companies and cost leaders call for EV/EBITDA, companies whose edge is reputation or brand for P/E, and companies with negative earnings or value tied to their customer base for EV/Sales or a customer-based driver. For a pre-revenue start-up none of the standard multiples works."}
    ],
    formulas: [
      {name:"Market capitalisation and enterprise value",expression:"E = price × shares outstanding · EV = E + financial debt − cash",detail:"Simplified formula from the lectures. Use consistent values in the same currency. With net cash, equity value can exceed EV."},
      {name:"Applying the multiple",expression:"Target EV = peers’ EV/metric multiple × target metric",detail:"For an equity-side multiple, the product gives equity value directly. Keep the same reference period for the denominator."},
      {name:"Price/earnings",expression:"P/E = price per share / EPS = market cap / profit attributable to shareholders",detail:"The equality requires the same attributable profit and the same share base for EPS and market cap. If the weighted average shares used for EPS differ from the shares outstanding today, the two ratios can diverge. Also check dilution effects."},
      {name:"PEG",expression:"PEG = (P/E) / expected annual EPS growth in percentage points",detail:"Under the course convention, for 12% growth you put 12 in the denominator. Check how reliable the forecast is and how risky the peers are."},
      {name:"Price/book value",expression:"P/BV = market cap / book equity attributable to shareholders",detail:"A result above 1 means market value exceeds book equity. Asset valuation rules affect the comparison."},
      {name:"Annualised EPS growth",expression:"g = (final EPS / initial EPS)^(1/n) − 1",detail:"n counts the annual intervals between the two EPS figures. With positive values, the CAGR gives the equivalent compound annual rate."}
    ],
    questions: [
      {id:"mul-01",type:"choice",level:1,prompt:"A company wants to estimate its value with EV/EBITDA. What does it get by multiplying the target’s EBITDA by the peers’ multiple?",options:["The price of a single share","Expected net profit","The value of the firm to all capital providers","Book equity only"],answer:2,explanation:"The multiple’s numerator is enterprise value. Multiplying by EBITDA gives an EV. With the course bridge, equity is obtained by subtracting net financial debt."},
      {id:"mul-02",type:"number",level:1,context:"A listed company has 8 million shares priced at €12, financial debt of 47 million and cash of 19 million. Use the course’s simplified bridge.",prompt:"Calculate enterprise value.",unit:"million €",answer:124,tolerance:0.01,explanation:"Market cap is 8 × 12 = 96 million. Net debt is 47 − 19 = 28 million. EV is therefore 96 + 28 = 124 million."},
      {id:"mul-03",type:"multi",level:1,prompt:"Which ratios have a numerator and a denominator that refer to the same capital providers?",options:["EV / shareholders’ net profit","EV / EBIT","Market cap / FCFE","EV / FCFF"],answer:[1,2,3],explanation:"EBIT and FCFF are calculated before debt flows and can be paired with EV. FCFE belongs to shareholders and goes with market cap. Shareholders’ net profit is an equity-side figure."},
      {id:"mul-04",type:"order",level:1,prompt:"Order the steps of relative valuation following the sequence from the lectures.",options:["Apply the multiple to the target’s metric","Identify comparable companies","Choose the most representative of the available multiples","Define which multiples can be calculated from the available data"],answer:[1,3,2,0],explanation:"First you build an economically comparable sample. You check which ratios can be calculated and justify the choice based on value drivers. Only then do you apply the multiple to the target."},
      {id:"mul-05",type:"number",level:2,context:"Two comparable manufacturers have EVs of 90 and 240 million and EBITDA of 15 and 30 million respectively. The target’s EBITDA is 24 million. Use the arithmetic mean of the individual multiples.",prompt:"What EV do you estimate for the target?",unit:"million €",answer:168,tolerance:0.01,explanation:"The peers trade at 90/15 = 6× and 240/30 = 8×. The arithmetic mean is 7×. Applying it to the target’s EBITDA gives 7 × 24 = 168 million."},
      {id:"mul-06",type:"number",level:1,context:"A peer’s share trades at €32 and its earnings per share for the last financial year are €2. The target’s profit for the same year, entirely attributable to ordinary shareholders, is 9 million.",prompt:"Using the peer’s historical P/E, what is the target’s equity worth?",unit:"million €",answer:144,tolerance:0.01,explanation:"The P/E is 32/2 = 16×. The denominator is profit attributable to shareholders, so 16 × 9 = 144 million is already an estimate of equity."},
      {id:"mul-07",type:"choice",level:2,context:"A platform collects recurring subscriptions from European customers. Which potential peer deserves priority, size being equal?",prompt:"Choose the comparison with the best-aligned value drivers.",options:["A server maker that sells one-off hardware","A European subscription platform with similar churn and growth","A European company that earns all its revenue from advertising","The group with the most similar trade name"],answer:1,explanation:"Recurring revenue, churn rate and growth determine how long customer relationships last and what they are worth. The same geographic area also makes competitive conditions more comparable."},
      {id:"mul-08",type:"number",level:2,context:"Peers trade on average at €1,200 of EV per paying subscriber. The target has 18,000 paying subscribers, 40,000 registered accounts and net financial debt of 3.6 million.",prompt:"What is the target’s equity value in € million?",unit:"million €",answer:18,tolerance:0.01,explanation:"Apply the multiple to the homogeneous metric: 1,200 × 18,000 = 21.6 million of EV. Subtract 3.6 million of net debt to get 18 million of equity."},
      {id:"mul-09",type:"choice",level:1,prompt:"A start-up has positive sales, negative EBITDA and net losses. Among the multiples listed, which one can still be applied using positive figures?",options:["Historical P/E","Historical EV/EBITDA","EV/Sales","P/E based on the same loss per share"],answer:2,explanation:"Positive revenue allows EV/Sales to be calculated. To interpret it, check whether future margins are sustainable. Two companies with the same revenue can generate very different cash flows."},
      {id:"mul-10",type:"multi",level:2,prompt:"You are comparing EV/EBITDA for industrial companies. Which checks improve the interpretation?",options:["That they all have the same number of shares","That EBITDA figures have a homogeneous operating scope","That investment intensity is comparable","That non-recurring items are treated consistently"],answer:[1,2,3],explanation:"Consistent scope and adjustments make the denominator comparable. EBITDA comes before investment, so different capex needs can justify different multiples even with similar margins."},
      {id:"mul-11",type:"number",level:2,context:"A company has a P/E of 21×. Expected EPS grows by 14% a year. Use the course convention, with growth expressed in percentage points.",prompt:"Calculate the PEG.",unit:"×",answer:1.5,tolerance:0.005,explanation:"The 14% growth enters the denominator as 14. PEG = 21/14 = 1.50. The measure should be read together with risk and the credibility of the expected growth."},
      {id:"mul-12",type:"number",level:2,context:"EPS is €2.00 today. Forecasts take it to €2.42 in exactly two years.",prompt:"What is the compound annual growth rate of EPS?",unit:"%",answer:10,tolerance:0.01,explanation:"The final/initial ratio is 2.42/2 = 1.21. Its square root is 1.10. Subtracting 1 gives a compound annual growth rate of 10%."},
      {id:"mul-13",type:"choice",level:2,prompt:"A peer has a forward P/E of 14×, calculated on next year’s expected profit. Which target figure should be multiplied by 14 to get a consistent estimate of its equity value?",options:["EBITDA for the last financial year","Net profit attributable to shareholders forecast for next year","Average revenue over the last three years","Current book value of equity"],answer:1,explanation:"The denominator of a forward P/E is shareholders’ expected profit. Using the target’s figure for the same year preserves both the scope and the time horizon of the comparison."},
      {id:"mul-14",type:"number",level:3,context:"Two independent divisions are valued with asset-side multiples. The services division has EBITDA of 14 million and peers at 8× EV/EBITDA. The software division has revenue of 25 million and peers at 3× EV/Sales. The group has cash of 21 and financial debt of 48 million. There are no other adjustments.",prompt:"Estimate consolidated equity value.",unit:"million €",answer:160,tolerance:0.01,explanation:"The divisional EVs are 14 × 8 = 112 and 25 × 3 = 75. Total EV = 187. Consolidated net debt is 48 − 21 = 27. Equity is 187 − 27 = 160 million."},
      {id:"mul-15",type:"number",level:3,context:"For division A, the peers’ P/E is 12× and profit is 5 million. For division B, the peers’ EV/EBITDA is 7×, EBITDA is 8 million and the net debt attributable to the division is 10 million. The divisions are autonomous and the holding company has no other debt or assets.",prompt:"Calculate total equity by adding up consistent values.",unit:"million €",answer:106,tolerance:0.01,explanation:"Division A is already worth 12 × 5 = 60 million of equity. Division B is worth 7 × 8 = 56 of EV, i.e. 56 − 10 = 46 of equity. The consistent sum is 60 + 46 = 106 million."},
      {id:"mul-16",type:"multi",level:3,prompt:"The target’s P/E is above the industry median. Which circumstances could justify the difference?",options:["Higher expected earnings growth","A lower return required by shareholders","Current profit temporarily depressed by an exceptional expense","More shares after a stock split, with unchanged total value and unchanged profit"],answer:[0,1,2],explanation:"More growth and lower risk can support a higher price per euro of profit. An exceptional expense depresses the denominator. A simple stock split changes price and EPS proportionally, leaving P/E unchanged."},
      {id:"mul-17",type:"number",level:2,context:"Three peers have P/BV ratios of 0.9×, 1.3× and 2.6× respectively. Following the course convention, use the simple average of the peers’ multiples, rounded to two decimals. The target’s equity attributable to ordinary shareholders is 75 million.",prompt:"What equity value does the average P/BV suggest?",unit:"million €",answer:120,tolerance:0.01,explanation:"The average multiple is (0.9 + 1.3 + 2.6) / 3 = 1.60×. Estimated equity is 1.60 × 75 = 120 million. A single high multiple such as 2.6× pulls the average up, which is why each peer’s comparability must be checked before averaging."},
      {id:"mul-18",type:"choice",level:3,context:"An industrial target reinvests far more than its peers every year to keep its plant running, with the same EBITDA, growth and operating risk.",prompt:"Which caveat matters most when applying EV/EBITDA mechanically?",options:["EBITDA ignores the higher capex required and can overvalue the target","Higher capex always increases the target’s P/E","EBITDA already includes all purchases of plant","The peers’ share counts make the comparison impossible"],answer:0,explanation:"With the same EBITDA, a greater reinvestment need reduces available cash. Applying the same multiple without reflecting this difference can give the target an excessive EV."},
      {id:"mul-19",type:"choice",exam:true,level:2,context:"Company F (k€): EBIT 5,000; net financial expenses 1,000; tax rate 25%; 2,000 shares. Peers in the same industry — price (€/share), EPS (€/share), D/E, growth of net cash flow: H 9.00, 1.50, 1.8, +8%; I 12.60, 1.80, 2.0, +10%; J 8.00, 2.00, 1.9, +9%; K 3.00, 1.50, 4.5, −20%.",prompt:"Using P/E and the course conventions (exclude non-comparable firms, average the multiples rounded to two decimals), what is the equity value of Company F?",options:["Around €17,010k","Around €14,250k","Around €22,680k","None of the other answers"],answer:0,explanation:"K is not comparable: much higher risk (D/E 4.5) and shrinking cash flows. P/E: H = 6.00, I = 7.00, J = 4.00, average 5.67. F’s net profit = (5,000 − 1,000) × 0.75 = 3,000, so equity = 5.67 × 3,000 = 17,010. Keeping K gives an average of 4.75 (14,250); applying the multiple to pre-tax profit gives 22,680."},
      {id:"mul-20",type:"choice",exam:true,level:2,context:"Company A makes bags, owns relevant tangible assets and competes on low production costs. Its EBITDA is €30m and its net financial debt €20m. Potential peers — EV (€m), EBITDA (€m), D/E, expected growth, competitive advantage: B 240, 40, 1.2, +2.5%, low cost; C 210, 30, 1.3, +2.2%, low cost; D 300, 20, 1.3, +1.8%, luxury brand for a niche; E 150, 25, 3.5, +9.0%, low cost.",prompt:"What is the most appropriate estimate of A’s equity value (market capitalisation)?",options:["Around €175m","Around €195m","Around €235m","Around €215m"],answer:0,explanation:"Tangible assets and cost leadership point to an asset-side multiple, EV/EBITDA. Only B and C are comparable: D has a different business model and E a different risk and growth profile. EV/EBITDA: B = 6.00, C = 7.00, average 6.50. EV of A = 6.50 × 30 = 195; equity = 195 − 20 = 175. 195 stops at the EV, 235 averages all four peers and 215 adds the debt instead of subtracting it."},
      {id:"mul-21",type:"choice",exam:true,level:1,context:"A med-tech start-up founded a year ago is developing its first device, expected on the market in two years. It has no revenues yet, negative EBITDA and net losses, and it has borrowed heavily to buy laboratory equipment.",prompt:"Which multiple should be preferred to value the start-up?",options:["EV/EBITDA, because the start-up has invested heavily in tangible equipment","P/E, because shareholders bear most of the start-up’s risk","EV/Sales, because revenue is less volatile than profit","None of the other answers"],answer:3,explanation:"With no revenues EV/Sales cannot be computed, and negative EBITDA and losses rule out EV/EBITDA and P/E. None of these multiples gives a meaningful value: the analyst needs a different driver linked to the start-up’s future value, or another valuation method."},
      {id:"mul-22",type:"choice",exam:true,level:2,context:"Two listed companies in the same industry and with similar risk: F has a P/E of 18× and expected EPS growth of 12% a year; D has a P/E of 15× and expected EPS growth of 6% a year.",prompt:"Using the course convention for the PEG, which statement is TRUE?",options:["F’s PEG (1.50) is lower than D’s (2.50): relative to its growth, F is cheaper","D’s PEG is lower than F’s, because D has the lower P/E","The two PEGs are equal, because both P/Es are above 10","None of the other answers"],answer:0,explanation:"PEG = P/E / growth in percentage points: F = 18 / 12 = 1.50 and D = 15 / 6 = 2.50. F’s higher P/E is more than justified by its faster growth, so per unit of growth investors pay less for F."},
      {id:"mul-23",type:"choice",exam:true,level:1,context:"A company reports net income of €30m, EBITDA of €60m, revenue of €240m, financial debts of €50m and cash of €14m, with 12 million shares outstanding. Comparable companies trade on average at a P/E of 14× and an EV/EBITDA of 8×.",prompt:"What is the share price based on the P/E multiple?",options:["€35.00","€37.00","€40.00","None of the other answers"],answer:0,explanation:"Equity value = 14 × 30 = €420m, so the share price = 420 / 12 = €35.00. €37.00 comes from EV/EBITDA (EV 480 − net debt 36 = equity 444); €40.00 divides the EV by the number of shares."},
      {id:"mul-24",type:"choice",exam:true,level:3,context:"An analyst estimated CARFRAMES’ enterprise value at €480.00m. CARFRAMES: revenues €800m, EBIT €60m, net profit €30m, net financial debt €80m. Peers’ average multiples: EV/Revenues 0.60×, EV/EBIT 8.50×, P/E 12.00×.",prompt:"Which multiple did the analyst use? Use two decimals.",options:["EV/Revenues","EV/EBIT","P/E, adding the net financial debt to reach the EV","None of the other answers"],answer:0,explanation:"EV/Revenues gives 0.60 × 800 = 480.00, the analyst’s figure. EV/EBIT gives 8.50 × 60 = 510.00. P/E gives equity of 12.00 × 30 = 360.00 and an EV of 360 + 80 = 440.00."},
      {id:"mul-25",type:"choice",exam:true,level:2,prompt:"Financial advisers often prefer relative valuation to DCF. Which of the following statements is WRONG?",options:["It requires fewer explicit forecasts than a DCF","Clients usually find it easier to understand than a DCF","It is unaffected by market sentiment, because it relies on accounting figures","Asset-side and equity-side multiples can lead to different equity values, so the choice of perspective matters"],answer:2,explanation:"Multiples combine accounting figures with current market prices, so if the market over- or undervalues the whole sector, the target inherits that mood. The other three statements are standard reasons for, and caveats about, using relative valuation."}
    ]
  }
);

window.AFC_TOPICS.push({
  id: "wacc",
  title: "WACC & Cost of Capital",
  description: "Cost of debt and equity, betas from comparables and the weighted average cost of capital, with the course conventions.",
  source: "Lectures 12 and 14 · Value measurement: DCF methods (cost of capital). Lecture 17 · Exercises on the cost of capital. Past-exam bank · section 2.2 WACC.",
  notes: [
    {title: "What the WACC measures", body: "The WACC is the return required by all providers of interest-bearing capital, weighted by their share of D + E, and it is the rate used to discount FCFF into enterprise value. In the course exercises D is financial debt and, unless the case explicitly asks for market values, E is book equity: share capital at nominal value plus reserves plus net income."},
    {title: "Cost of debt", body: "kd can be measured as interest expenses / financial debt, or built as the risk-free rate plus the credit default spread (CDS) that matches the company’s rating. Only the after-tax cost kd × (1 − t) enters the WACC, with t = income taxes / EBT. More leverage worsens the rating and raises the CDS."},
    {title: "Choosing rf and rm", body: "Use the 10-year government bond yield of the least risky issuer in the currency area where the company earns its cash flows (the German Bund for euro flows, US Treasuries for dollar flows), not the yield of its home country. Take rm from the stock index of the same market, for example the Euro Stoxx for the euro area or the S&P 500 for the US."},
    {title: "Beta for an unlisted company", body: "Select listed peers with the same business and markets and a similar D/E, and drop the outliers. Unlever each peer with its own tax rate and D/E, average the unlevered betas rounding to two decimals, then relever with the target’s D/E and tax rate. Because βL also carries financial risk, the levered beta is higher than the unlevered one whenever there is debt."},
    {title: "Reading what moves the WACC", body: "More debt adds a tax shield but also raises the CDS and the levered beta, so both kd and ke go up. Lease liabilities count as financial debt. The volatility of returns enters through beta. Being a weighted average of ke and the after-tax kd, the WACC always lies between the two."}
  ],
  formulas: [
    {name: "Weighted average cost of capital", expression: "WACC = ke × E/(D + E) + kd × (1 − t) × D/(D + E)", detail: "D = financial debt, E = book equity unless the case asks for market values. Round percentages to four decimals (e.g. 0.1247 = 12.47%)."},
    {name: "Cost of debt", expression: "kd = interest expenses / financial debt  ·  kd = rf + CDS", detail: "The CDS depends on the credit rating. Use the after-tax cost kd × (1 − t) in the WACC."},
    {name: "Effective tax rate", expression: "t = income taxes / EBT", detail: "Use the taxes in the income statement. If only net profit and taxes are given, EBT = net profit + taxes."},
    {name: "Book value of equity", expression: "E = shares × nominal value + reserves + net income", detail: "The convention of the course solutions for the WACC weights. Use market capitalisation only when the case explicitly asks for market values."},
    {name: "Unlevering and relevering beta", expression: "βU = βL / [1 + (1 − t) × D/E]  ·  βL = βU × [1 + (1 − t) × D/E]", detail: "Unlever each peer with its own t and D/E, average the βU (two decimals), then relever with the target’s t and D/E."},
    {name: "CAPM", expression: "ke = rf + βL × (rm − rf)", detail: "rf and rm come from the same currency area and market as the company’s cash flows."}
  ],
  questions: [
    {id:"wacc-01",type:"choice",exam:true,level:2,context:"Company D operates in the euro area. 2019 data (€): EBIT 40,000; net income 18,000; interest expenses 10,000; financial income 0; financial debt 200,000; reserves 70,000. The equity consists of 100,000 shares with a nominal value of €1.00, trading at €1.30 at year-end. The cost of equity is 6.0%. 10-year government bond yields: Germany 0.8%, France 1.2%, Italy 2.5%, US 2.1%.",prompt:"Following the course conventions, what is Company D’s WACC?",options:["Around 4.45%","Around 4.18%","Around 5.48%","None of the other answers"],answer:0,explanation:"EBT = 40,000 − 10,000 = 30,000, so taxes = 12,000 and t = 40%. Book equity = 100,000 × 1.00 + 70,000 + 18,000 = 188,000 and kd = 10,000 / 200,000 = 5%. WACC = 188/388 × 6% + 200/388 × 5% × 0.6 ≈ 4.45%. Market-value equity (130,000) gives 4.18%; ignoring the tax shield gives 5.48%. The bond yields are not needed, because ke is given."},
    {id:"wacc-02",type:"choice",exam:true,level:2,context:"Company A sells premium cosmetics in the euro area. It has bank debts of €600m, equity of €400m and a tax rate of 35%. The risk-free rate is 0.8%. Expected index returns: FTSE MIB 11%, Euro Stoxx 9%, S&P 500 13%. Potential peers: B sells gene-based drugs in emerging Asian markets (βU 0.95); C and D sell premium cosmetics in the euro area (βU 0.52 and 0.48).",prompt:"What is the best estimate of Company A’s cost of equity? Round betas to two decimals.",options:["Around 8.9%","Around 11.3%","Around 4.9%","Around 12.9%"],answer:0,explanation:"Only C and D are comparable: B sells different products in different markets. βU = (0.52 + 0.48) / 2 = 0.50 and βL = 0.50 × [1 + 0.65 × 600/400] = 0.99. With the Euro Stoxx as the market for euro-area sales: ke = 0.8% + 0.99 × (9% − 0.8%) ≈ 8.9%. Including B gives 11.3%, forgetting to relever gives 4.9% and using the S&P 500 gives 12.9%."},
    {id:"wacc-03",type:"choice",exam:true,level:3,context:"Beta Ltd sells all its products in the US. Total assets $20m; equity $6m; non-financial liabilities $5m; income tax rate 30%. Its BBB rating implies a credit default spread of 2.00%. 10-year government bond yields: China 2.7%, US 1.5%, Germany 1.2%. Index returns: CSI 300 (China) 12%, S&P 500 (US) 10%, DAX (Germany) 9%. Industry averages: equity beta 1.10, D/E 1.00 (D = financial liabilities), tax rate 25%.",prompt:"What is the WACC of Beta Ltd? Round numbers to two decimals and percentages to four decimals (e.g. 0.1247 = 12.47%).",options:["Around 6.46%","Around 7.85%","Around 5.85%","None of the other answers"],answer:0,explanation:"Financial debt = 20 − 6 − 5 = 9, so D/E = 1.5. Industry βU = 1.10 / [1 + 0.75 × 1.00] = 0.63 and Beta’s βL = 0.63 × [1 + 0.70 × 1.5] = 1.29. Sales are in dollars, so rf = 1.5% (US) and rm = 10% (S&P 500): kd = 1.5% + 2.0% = 3.5% and ke = 1.5% + 1.29 × 8.5% = 12.47%. WACC = 12.47% × 6/15 + 3.5% × 0.7 × 9/15 ≈ 6.46%. Chinese data give 7.85% and German data 5.85%."},
    {id:"wacc-04",type:"choice",exam:true,level:3,context:"Company B is based in the US and is not listed. Balance sheet ($m): equity 300; bank debts 450; payables 150. Income statement ($m): EBIT 90; financial costs 27; EBT 63; net income 44.1. Its WACC is 7% and the risk-free rate 2%. Comparable companies — bank debts, equity, βL, tax rate: X 400, 250, 1.20, 35%; Y 640, 400, 1.12, 30%.",prompt:"What expected market return is consistent with these data? Round betas to two decimals.",options:["Around 10.0%","Around 8.9%","Around 18.4%","None of the other answers"],answer:0,explanation:"kd = 27 / 450 = 6% and t = (63 − 44.1) / 63 = 30%. From the WACC: 7% = 300/750 × ke + 450/750 × 6% × 0.7, so ke = 11.2%. βU: X = 1.20 / [1 + 0.65 × 1.6] = 0.59 and Y = 1.12 / [1 + 0.70 × 1.6] = 0.53, average 0.56; B’s βL = 0.56 × [1 + 0.70 × 1.5] = 1.15. CAPM: 11.2% = 2% + 1.15 × (rm − 2%), so rm = 10%. Counting payables as debt gives 8.9%; using βU gives 18.4%."},
    {id:"wacc-05",type:"choice",exam:true,level:2,context:"For a manufacturing company in 2019 you know: WACC 6.5%; ke 7.6%; asset turnover ratio 0.4; interest coverage ratio (EBIT / interest expenses) 8. Revenues €40,000; cost of goods sold €14,000; SG&A €16,000 (D&A are included in these costs); bank debt €25,000, which is the only liability.",prompt:"What is the company’s corporate tax rate?",options:["Around 36%","Around 64%","Around 58%","There is not enough information to calculate it"],answer:0,explanation:"EBIT = 40,000 − 14,000 − 16,000 = 10,000, so interest = 10,000 / 8 = 1,250 and kd = 1,250 / 25,000 = 5%. Total assets = 40,000 / 0.4 = 100,000, so E = 75,000 and the weights are 75% and 25%. 6.5% = 75% × 7.6% + 25% × 5% × (1 − t) gives 1 − t = 0.64, so t = 36%. 64% is 1 − t; 58% comes from treating total assets as equity."},
    {id:"wacc-06",type:"choice",exam:true,level:3,context:"Hannover is an unlisted Italian company: equity €400m; financial debts €600m; cost of financial debts 4%; tax rate 30%; WACC 7.48%. Index returns: FTSE MIB 12%, DAX 11%, S&P 500 15%. Potential peers — financial debt, equity, βL, tax rate: 1) 600, 400, 1.20, 30%; 2) 750, 500, 1.30, 30%; 3) 900, 300, 0.80, 30%.",prompt:"Which risk-free rate was used to compute Hannover’s cost of capital? Round betas to two decimals.",options:["Around 2.0%","Around 17.0%","Around 9.2%","None of the other answers"],answer:0,explanation:"From the WACC: 7.48% = 0.4 × ke + 0.6 × 4% × 0.7, so ke = 14.5%. Peer 3 has a much higher D/E (3.0 against 1.5), so only peers 1 and 2 count: βU = 0.59 and 0.63, average 0.61, and Hannover’s βL = 0.61 × [1 + 0.7 × 1.5] = 1.25. With the FTSE MIB for an Italian company: 14.5% = rf + 1.25 × (12% − rf), so rf = 2%. The S&P 500 would give 17%, and ignoring the tax shield 9.2%."},
    {id:"wacc-07",type:"choice",exam:true,level:1,prompt:"A colleague makes four claims about the WACC. Which one is WRONG?",options:["Switching from buying machines to financial leasing changes the WACC, because lease liabilities are financial debt","A rating downgrade raises the WACC through a higher credit default spread","The tax shield pushes the WACC below the after-tax cost of debt","A company whose returns swing more with the market has a higher WACC, all else being equal"],answer:2,explanation:"The WACC is a weighted average of ke and the after-tax kd, so it always lies between them: the tax shield lowers the cost of debt, but the WACC stays above it. Leases are financial debt, the CDS enters kd and the sensitivity of returns to the market enters ke through beta."},
    {id:"wacc-08",type:"choice",exam:true,level:1,prompt:"You must estimate the cost of equity of an unlisted company with the CAPM. Which input do you need?",options:["Its market capitalisation, because beta is estimated from the company’s own share price","Its credit default spread, because shareholders bear default risk","Its D/E and tax rate, to relever the average unlevered beta of listed peers","Its historical payout ratio, because ke equals the dividend yield"],answer:2,explanation:"An unlisted company has no share price, so βL is rebuilt from the peers’ unlevered beta and the company’s own D/E and tax rate. The CDS belongs to the cost of debt, and the CAPM does not use dividends."},
    {id:"wacc-09",type:"choice",exam:true,level:2,prompt:"Company A will sharply increase its leverage by taking new bank loans, with no change in equity. What should happen to its cost of equity?",options:["It rises, because the banks raise the credit default spread they charge","It rises, because the levered beta increases","It falls, because the tax shield on the new interest lowers the cost of capital","It does not change, because ke depends only on rf and the market premium"],answer:1,explanation:"With more debt, shareholders bear more financial risk: βL = βU × [1 + (1 − t) × D/E] goes up, and so does ke. A higher CDS raises the cost of debt, not ke, and the tax shield works on the after-tax cost of debt."},
    {id:"wacc-10",type:"choice",exam:true,level:1,prompt:"The listed peers of an unlisted target all carry significant financial debt. How does each peer’s unlevered beta compare with its levered beta?",options:["The unlevered beta is lower","The unlevered beta is higher","They are identical","It depends on the level of debt: it can be higher or lower"],answer:0,explanation:"βL = βU × [1 + (1 − t) × D/E], and the factor in brackets is above 1 whenever there is debt. Removing the financial risk therefore always lowers the beta: βU < βL."},
    {id:"wacc-11",type:"choice",exam:true,level:1,context:"A company based in Milan sells all its products in the euro area. 10-year government bond yields: Italy 3.6%, France 2.9%, Germany 2.4%, US 4.1%.",prompt:"Which risk-free rate should you use in its cost of capital?",options:["2.4%, the German yield","3.6%, the yield of its home country","4.1%, the yield of the largest bond market","2.97%, the average of the euro-area yields"],answer:0,explanation:"The course takes the yield of the least risky government bond in the currency area of the company’s cash flows. For euro cash flows that is the German Bund, wherever the company is based."},
    {id:"wacc-12",type:"choice",exam:true,level:1,context:"A company rated BBB pays a credit default spread of 2.5%. The risk-free rate is 2.0% and the tax rate 24%.",prompt:"What after-tax cost of debt enters its WACC?",options:["Around 3.42%","Around 4.50%","Around 1.90%","Around 2.50%"],answer:0,explanation:"kd = rf + CDS = 2.0% + 2.5% = 4.5%. After tax: 4.5% × (1 − 24%) = 3.42%. 4.50% is the pre-tax cost, 1.90% taxes only the spread and 2.50% is the spread alone."}
  ]
});
